## Attribute every row to its author — REQUIRED

Every table that stores visitor-created or visitor-submitted data (scores,
posts, comments, votes, saves, messages, uploads — anything a request wrote)
**must** include a `user_id TEXT` column holding the visitor's
`x-websim-user-id`, including when you also store `username` for display.
Do this even for tables where per-user lookup isn't needed.

This is a moderation contract, not a style preference: when a user is banned
from this project or from websim entirely, **the platform automatically
deletes every row whose `user_id` column matches theirs, in every table that
has one**. A table without `user_id` cannot be cleaned up, so a banned
user's spam or abuse would stay in the project forever. Consequences:

- Name the column exactly `user_id` (not `author_id`, `owner`, `uid`).
  Rows the platform should never delete (project config, aggregate counters,
  content created by `server.js` itself) belong in tables WITHOUT a
  `user_id` column.
- Store denormalized `username` only alongside `user_id` in the same row,
  so it disappears with the row.
- Don't write signed-out visitors' content into durable tables — there is no
  id to attribute it to, so it can never be scrubbed. Require sign-in for
  writes (as the example below does), or keep anonymous input ephemeral.
- Expect attributed rows to vanish: don't make other users' data reference a
  row in a way that breaks hard when it's deleted.

## env.DB — the project's private SQLite database

`env.DB` is a SQLite database that belongs to this project alone. It follows
the Cloudflare D1 API for the methods listed here — `prepare`, `bind`,
`all`, `first`, `run`, `raw`, `batch`, and `exec`. Other D1 methods
(`withSession`, `dump`) do not exist, and `meta.duration` is always 0.

```js
// Read many rows
const { results } = await env.DB
  .prepare("SELECT * FROM scores WHERE game = ? ORDER BY points DESC LIMIT 10")
  .bind("main")
  .all();

// Read one row (or one column of it)
const row = await env.DB.prepare("SELECT * FROM scores WHERE user_id = ?").bind(userId).first();
const best = await env.DB.prepare("SELECT MAX(points) AS p FROM scores").first("p");

// Write
const { meta } = await env.DB
  .prepare("INSERT INTO scores (user_id, points) VALUES (?, ?)")
  .bind(userId, 42)
  .run();
// meta.last_row_id, meta.changes

// Several writes, all-or-nothing (one transaction)
await env.DB.batch([
  env.DB.prepare("INSERT INTO scores (user_id, points) VALUES (?, ?)").bind(userId, 42),
  env.DB.prepare("UPDATE totals SET plays = plays + 1 WHERE id = ?").bind(userId),
]);

```

Tables: export your schema and it is applied once, before the first request
or room event — no CREATE TABLE in handlers.

```js
export const schema = `
  CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS scores (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, user_id TEXT, points INTEGER);
  CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS scores_user ON scores (user_id);
`;
```

- SQLite dialect (not Postgres): `INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT`,
  `TEXT`, `REAL`, `BLOB`; `?` placeholders via `.bind()`. Always use
  `.bind()` for values — never interpolate strings into SQL.
- `schema` must be idempotent (`IF NOT EXISTS`): it runs on every cold
  start against a database that already has your tables.
- The database **persists across edits and new versions** of the project. A
  remix/fork of the project starts with its own empty database. No other
  project can ever read it.
- There is a size limit (512 MB). Writes past it fail while reads and deletes
  keep working, so store URLs and ids rather than large blobs — put the bytes
  in `env.BLOB`.
- File bytes cannot go in the database at all: each bound parameter is capped
  at 128 KB (blob bytes or string characters) and SQL text at 100 KB, so
  images/audio/video — raw or base64 — are refused. Store them with
  `env.BLOB.put()` and save the returned URL in the database.
- String values being written pass a profanity filter and are stored with any
  flagged words censored (`***`); don't rely on written text coming back
  byte-for-byte identical.

## env.AI — websim-hosted inference (no API keys)

Server-side AI without any provider account or key:

```js
// Chat completion (fast general model). Returns { content: string }.
const { content } = await env.AI.chat({
  messages: [
    { role: "system", content: "You are a pirate. Answer in one sentence." },
    { role: "user", content: userQuestion },
  ],
});

// Ask for JSON and get a parseable string back
const { content: jsonText } = await env.AI.chat({
  messages: [{ role: "user", content: "List 3 fruits as a JSON array" }],
  json: true,
});
const fruits = JSON.parse(jsonText);

// Image generation. Returns { url } — a permanent public image URL.
const { url } = await env.AI.generateImage({
  prompt: "a watercolor fox, minimalist",
  aspectRatio: "1:1", // optional; also accepts width/height, seed, transparent
});
```

- Use `env.AI` for server-authoritative AI (NPC turns, judging submissions,
  content that must be generated once and shared). For purely client-side AI
  chat, the `window.websim` client API is still the simplest path.
- Prompts pass content moderation, and calls are rate limited per visitor, per
  project, and per project per day — image generation much more tightly than
  chat. Treat a rate-limit error as expected: catch it and return a useful
  message rather than letting the route 500. Never call `env.AI` in a loop
